WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.340
The central quantum concern for TLS planning is that some widely used public-key algorithms are considered vulnerable to future cryptographically relevant quantum computers.

2
00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:19.601
RSA, finite-field Diffie-Hellman, elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman, and ECDSA appear across many web and service environments.

3
00:00:19.601 --> 00:00:23.657
This does not mean every TLS endpoint is instantly unsafe today.

4
00:00:23.657 --> 00:00:32.594
It means teams should identify where these mechanisms protect information that must remain trustworthy or confidential over a long period.

5
00:00:32.594 --> 00:00:36.499
Symmetric encryption and hashing are affected differently.

6
00:00:36.499 --> 00:00:43.859
They are still part of quantum risk discussions, but the migration pressure is not identical to the public-key case.

7
00:00:43.859 --> 00:00:52.420
In TLS, the public-key portion of the handshake is what makes captured traffic and future decryption scenarios especially important.

8
00:00:52.420 --> 00:01:02.108
The application data is protected with symmetric keys, but the way those keys were established may become the weak link for long-lived confidentiality.

9
00:01:02.108 --> 00:01:14.049
Prioritization should consider data lifetime, confidentiality sensitivity, internet exposure, operational criticality, dependency complexity, and business or regulatory impact.

10
00:01:14.049 --> 00:01:19.081
Public-facing endpoints carrying sensitive data usually deserve attention.

11
00:01:19.081 --> 00:01:32.749
So do government and enterprise APIs, machine-to-machine services, mobile backends, internal service mesh traffic with sensitive business data, and partner integrations that cross organizational boundaries.

12
00:01:32.749 --> 00:01:38.532
A static brochure site has a different risk profile than an API carrying regulated records.

13
00:01:38.532 --> 00:01:42.437
Not every endpoint has the same urgency, and that is good news.

14
00:01:42.437 --> 00:01:47.544
A mature program can rank systems instead of trying to change everything at once.

15
00:01:47.544 --> 00:01:59.259
Teams should identify high-value traffic, long-lived data, externally exposed services, clients that are hard to update, and dependencies controlled by vendors or cloud providers.

16
00:01:59.259 --> 00:02:07.671
Prioritization turns PQC from a vague concern into a sequenced roadmap that engineering and operations teams can actually execute.
